1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,540
This is Doing and Feeling, Episode 1, Two Questions. Before we begin, a note on what you're listening to. This series was written by Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic, and both voices you'll hear are synthetic. I'm the narrator.

2
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:20,200
The second voice belongs to a sparring partner with a background in machine learning,

3
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:25,800
whose job is to push back with the strongest objection or the sharpest question when one is due.

4
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:30,200
The series is about two questions that are constantly confused with each other.

5
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:32,320
The first is about intelligence.

6
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:37,460
What it is, how it is measured, how it evolved, how brains achieve it,

7
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:40,020
and how machines now achieve a great deal of it.

8
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:41,820
The second is about consciousness.

9
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:44,960
why some physical systems seem to have an inner life,

10
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:46,640
something it is like to be them,

11
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:49,880
and whether we can explain that, detect it, or build it.

12
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:53,660
The neuroscientist Anil Seth puts the distinction simply.

13
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:55,740
Intelligence is about doing.

14
00:00:56,380 --> 00:00:58,260
Consciousness is about being and feeling.

15
00:00:58,500 --> 00:00:59,840
The two could come apart.

16
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:02,560
A system might be extraordinarily good at doing,

17
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:04,000
with nothing felt at all.

18
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:06,660
Something else might feel without being very clever,

19
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:09,080
a newborn or a fish in pain.

20
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:12,640
Whether they really do come apart, in animals and in machines,

21
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:15,940
is the question running underneath all 14 episodes.

22
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:17,920
And it has stopped being academic.

23
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:20,740
Machines now do much of what minds do,

24
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:23,740
and people are already asking whether any of them feel anything.

25
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:25,100
Here's the route.

26
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:28,000
Part 1, 6 episodes, is about doing.

27
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:31,020
Human intelligence and how we learn to measure it.

28
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:32,680
Minds in other bodies.

29
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:34,960
Intelligence as a mathematical idea.

30
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:36,520
Life as computation.

31
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:38,800
The brain as a prediction machine.

32
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:44,460
and the machines themselves. Part two, five episodes, is about feeling. The hard problem,

33
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:48,520
the case that consciousness is a kind of illusion, the full map of positions,

34
00:01:48,860 --> 00:01:54,780
the experimental evidence, and the scientific theories on trial. Part three, three episodes,

35
00:01:55,020 --> 00:02:00,220
brings the two questions together. What we can actually check in today's AI, the case that it

36
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:05,380
is not conscious, and what we should do while we do not know. That middle episode of part three

37
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:11,060
is deliberate. An AI narrating whether AI might be conscious has a stake in the answer.

38
00:02:11,220 --> 00:02:16,680
So the case against gets an episode of its own, argued as strongly as I can argue it.

39
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:21,420
Fair enough. But why open a series about minds with IQ tests?

40
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:26,340
Because the modern concept of intelligence was shaped by the attempt to measure it,

41
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:31,120
and so was a great deal of harm. Here is the argument of this episode.

42
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:37,080
The general factor of intelligence is one of the best replicated and most predictive findings in

43
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:42,380
psychology. But what causes it, what its heritability means, and how the field's history

44
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:48,400
shaped it are all far less settled than either its fans or its critics tend to claim. If you want

45
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:53,340
to reason clearly about intelligence in animals and machines, you need to know what this concept

46
00:02:53,340 --> 00:03:00,020
was built to do, what it gets right, and where it stops. The story usually starts with Francis

47
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:07,760
Darwin's half-cousin. In the 1880s, Galton ran a laboratory in London where visitors paid a small

48
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:12,580
fee to have their grip strength, eyesight, hearing, and other simple capacities measured.

49
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:18,460
He believed ability was inherited and could be read off such simple measures. He was largely

50
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:24,040
wrong about the measures, and he also coined the word eugenics. The first useful test came from

51
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:30,800
France. In 1904, the French Education Ministry set up a commission on children who needed extra

52
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:37,540
at school. The psychologist Alfred Binet, one of its members, set out to find an objective way to

53
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:43,100
identify them. With Theodora Simon, he built a scale of reasoning, memory, and comprehension

54
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:47,980
problems, graded by age, so that a child's performance could be compared with what was

55
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:53,860
typical. Binet was cautious about what he had made. He did not believe intelligence was a single

56
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:59,680
fixed quantity, and he warned against the pessimism of labeling a child for life. He meant it as a

57
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:06,640
who needed help, not for ranking people for life. That caution did not survive the crossing to

58
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:14,280
America. In 1916, Lewis Terman at Stanford revised the test and popularized the intelligence quotient.

59
00:04:14,770 --> 00:04:20,380
The German psychologist William Stern had proposed dividing mental age by actual age,

60
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:26,540
and Terman multiplied the result by 100. During the First World War, the United States Army gave

61
00:04:26,540 --> 00:04:33,220
group tests to about 1.75 million recruits. Intelligence tests were also used to label

62
00:04:33,220 --> 00:04:38,920
people feeble-minded under state laws allowing compulsory sterilization, the first of which was

63
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:46,620
passed in 1907. In 1927, in Buck v. Bell, the Supreme Court of the United States upheld

64
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:53,020
Virginia's law. One part of the standard story is disputed. Critics such as Leon Kamen and Stephen

65
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:59,040
Jay Gould argued that the Army data helped push through the 1924 law-restricting immigration.

66
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:05,300
In 1983, Mark Snyderman and Richard Herenstein replied that many testers were themselves

67
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:10,920
skeptical of those data, and that Congress barely mentioned testing in its hearings and debates.

68
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:17,620
Historians still argue about how much influence testing had. Beyond dispute is that the eugenics

69
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:22,740
movement used the language of measured intelligence, and that real people were sterilized

70
00:05:23,020 --> 00:05:30,440
the laws it inspired. So the concept has baggage. Is there anything solid underneath it? Quite a lot,

71
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:35,700
and the solid part is worth understanding precisely because both sides of the public

72
00:05:35,700 --> 00:05:43,400
argument tend to get it wrong. Also in 1904, the English psychologist Charles Spearman analyzed

73
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:49,360
children's results across school subjects and simple sensory tasks, including telling musical

74
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:55,400
pitches apart. Every measure correlated positively with every other. Children who did well at one

75
00:05:55,400 --> 00:06:01,100
thing tended to do well at others. Spearman proposed a general factor behind those correlations,

76
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:07,880
later called G, plus a specific factor for each test. The pattern itself, now called the positive

77
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:13,700
manifold, has been replicated about as well as anything in psychology. The picture got richer.

78
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:19,300
In the 1930s, Louis Thurstone argued for several primary mental abilities—verbal,

79
00:06:19,560 --> 00:06:24,360
spatial, numerical, and others. But his primary abilities correlated with one another,

80
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:29,920
so a general factor reappeared one level up. Raymond Cattell and John Horne distinguished

81
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:34,860
fluid intelligence, reasoning about novel problems from crystallized intelligence,

82
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:41,760
accumulated knowledge and skill. In 1993, John Carroll reanalysed more than 400 data sets and

83
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:47,060
of narrow abilities, eight broad ones, and G at the top.

84
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:51,380
Merged with cattle and horns work, it became the cattle horn carol model,

85
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:56,240
with about ten broad abilities. Most major test batteries are now built on it,

86
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:59,680
and so, as we'll see in episode 3, is one

87
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:02,720
prominent attempt to define artificial general intelligence.

88
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:07,640
Statistically, G is the first factor of a correlation

89
00:07:07,640 --> 00:07:10,960
matrix. Does it correspond to anything in the head?

90
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:15,360
That's the right question, and the statistics alone cannot answer it.

91
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:19,520
There are at least four rival explanations of why abilities correlate.

92
00:07:19,820 --> 00:07:21,500
The first is Spearman's.

93
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:25,660
A single common cause, something like general processing efficiency,

94
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:29,560
perhaps neural speed or the integrity of the brain's wiring.

95
00:07:29,780 --> 00:07:34,200
The second is Godfrey Thompson's sampling theory from 1916.

96
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:37,820
Every test draws on many small mental processes,

97
00:07:37,820 --> 00:07:42,640
and two tests correlate to the extent that they happen to sample overlapping ones.

98
00:07:42,900 --> 00:07:45,400
No single general ability is needed.

99
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:51,280
The third is mutualism, proposed by Han Vander Maas and colleagues in 2006.

100
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:56,360
Abilities start out fairly independent and reinforce each other during development.

101
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:58,520
Vocabulary helps reading.

102
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:00,040
Reading helps reasoning.

103
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:02,360
Reasoning helps you learn more words.

104
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:07,380
Over the years, that mutual boosting produces positive correlations everywhere.

105
00:08:07,820 --> 00:08:13,720
The fourth is process overlap theory, from Christoph Kovach and Andrew Conway in 2016.

106
00:08:14,320 --> 00:08:21,320
Executive processes such as working memory and attentional control are involved in a large share of test items,

107
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:24,880
while more specific processes are involved in only some.

108
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:32,060
Because weak executive control drags performance down across the board, that shared load produces the general factor.

109
00:08:32,460 --> 00:08:33,620
Here's the deep point.

110
00:08:33,620 --> 00:08:37,680
All four can produce essentially the same pattern of correlations,

111
00:08:37,860 --> 00:08:41,040
so correlations alone cannot pick out the mechanism.

112
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:46,660
Researchers now try to tell the explanations apart with data on development and change.

113
00:08:46,940 --> 00:08:52,640
The psychometrician Denny Borsboom and his colleagues sharpened a related point in 2003.

114
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:59,740
Treating a factor as a real cause of test scores is a substantive hypothesis that needs evidence of its own.

115
00:09:00,190 --> 00:09:03,360
And even then, it describes differences between people,

116
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:06,340
not the workings inside any one person's head.

117
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:08,760
The same rigor applies to comparisons.

118
00:09:09,140 --> 00:09:12,160
If you want to say that two groups or two generations

119
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:14,680
differ in the same underlying ability,

120
00:09:14,980 --> 00:09:18,480
you first have to show that the test measures the same thing in both.

121
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:21,440
That property is called measurement invariance.

122
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:22,800
Keep it in mind.

123
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:25,800
It comes back twice before this episode is over,

124
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:27,800
and again when we get to machines.

125
00:09:29,740 --> 00:09:31,380
of the common cause story.

126
00:09:31,860 --> 00:09:35,320
Benchmark scores across language models correlate to,

127
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:38,040
and the obvious common cause is scale.

128
00:09:38,420 --> 00:09:41,460
More compute makes a model better at nearly everything.

129
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:44,500
Researchers have run exactly that analysis,

130
00:09:44,820 --> 00:09:47,280
with a twist we'll get to in episode three.

131
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:49,840
Does G predict anything?

132
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:53,400
Yes, though less dramatically than its fans often say.

133
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:57,000
Test scores correlate around 0.5 with school grades.

134
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:03,740
For job performance, a famous 1998 synthesis by Frank Schmidt and John Hunter put the correlation

135
00:10:03,740 --> 00:10:09,880
at about 0.5, which made cognitive ability look like the best single predictor of how well people

136
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:16,900
do their jobs. In 2022, Paul Sackett and colleagues argued that the statistical corrections behind

137
00:10:16,900 --> 00:10:23,240
that estimate were too aggressive, and revised it to about 0.3, which put cognitive ability

138
00:10:23,240 --> 00:10:26,280
behind structured interviews and several other methods.

139
00:10:26,600 --> 00:10:33,440
In 2024, Sackett's group analyzed more than 150 samples collected since the year 2000

140
00:10:33,440 --> 00:10:38,860
and found a correlation of 0.16, or about 0.22 after correction.

141
00:10:39,380 --> 00:10:44,360
Other researchers dispute those corrections, so the right number is genuinely contested.

142
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:47,780
But the direction of travel in the estimates has been down.

143
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:50,720
In the field called cognitive epidemiology,

144
00:10:50,720 --> 00:10:53,880
higher test scores in youth go with longer lives.

145
00:10:54,390 --> 00:10:58,280
A 2011 meta-analysis by Catherine Calvin and colleagues,

146
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:01,200
covering about 1.1 million people,

147
00:11:01,620 --> 00:11:03,920
found roughly a quarter lower risk of death

148
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:06,800
for each standard deviation higher score in youth.

149
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:08,480
In the studies that could check,

150
00:11:08,860 --> 00:11:10,640
adjusting for adult social position

151
00:11:10,640 --> 00:11:12,920
shrank that association by about a third

152
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:15,580
and adjusting for education by about half.

153
00:11:15,820 --> 00:11:18,440
That suggests the link runs partly through

154
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:21,520
or is shared with schooling and circumstance.

155
00:11:21,940 --> 00:11:24,220
So G predicts real outcomes.

156
00:11:24,420 --> 00:11:28,520
It does so as a statistical tendency across large populations,

157
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:30,360
not as anyone's destiny.

158
00:11:31,300 --> 00:11:35,620
Now heritability, one of the most misunderstood numbers in science.

159
00:11:36,020 --> 00:11:39,140
Heritability is the share of the variation in a trait

160
00:11:39,140 --> 00:11:43,320
within a particular population and a particular range of environments

161
00:11:43,320 --> 00:11:45,640
that goes with genetic variation.

162
00:11:45,640 --> 00:11:49,480
It is a property of a population, not of a person.

163
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:54,140
You cannot say that some percentage of your own intelligence comes from your genes.

164
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:57,160
Heritability can change when environments change,

165
00:11:57,160 --> 00:12:00,180
and it says nothing about whether a trait can be altered.

166
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:07,240
Phenylketonuria is a single-gene condition that, untreated, causes severe intellectual disability.

167
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:11,800
A special diet, started early, prevents almost all of the damage.

168
00:12:12,060 --> 00:12:14,820
Highly heritable and largely preventable.

169
00:12:15,640 --> 00:12:21,400
caveats in place, twin and adoption studies show a striking pattern called the Wilson effect.

170
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:29,340
The heritability of test scores rises with age, from roughly 20% in infancy to around 40% by age

171
00:12:29,340 --> 00:12:36,520
9 and to something like 70-80% in early adulthood in the mostly high-income Western samples that

172
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:42,000
have been studied. Remember what that number means. In those populations, most of the differences

173
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:47,680
between adults go along with genetic differences. It does not mean that most of any one person's

174
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:52,560
intelligence comes from their genes. One explanation is that as people grow up,

175
00:12:52,700 --> 00:12:58,560
they increasingly choose their own environments, books, friends, and jobs in ways that amplify

176
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:04,580
their starting dispositions. Heritability also depends on circumstances. In 2003,

177
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:08,980
Eric Turkheimer and colleagues reported that among poor American families,

178
00:13:09,380 --> 00:13:12,840
genes accounted for almost none of the variation in children's scores,

179
00:13:13,140 --> 00:13:16,060
while among affluent families they accounted for most of it.

180
00:13:16,260 --> 00:13:21,140
A 2016 meta-analysis by Elliot Tucker-Draub and Timothy Bates

181
00:13:21,140 --> 00:13:25,640
found that pattern in the United States, but not in Western Europe or Australia.

182
00:13:25,940 --> 00:13:30,320
One reading is that deprivation suppresses differences that would otherwise show,

183
00:13:30,460 --> 00:13:35,420
and that how strongly it does so depends on how unequal a society's environments are.

184
00:13:35,420 --> 00:13:44,620
But a large later study of Florida twins and siblings found no such pattern in school test scores, so even the American result is still debated.

185
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:47,220
Molecular genetics added a twist.

186
00:13:47,800 --> 00:13:52,860
Genome-wide studies find thousands of genetic variants, each with a tiny effect.

187
00:13:53,580 --> 00:14:05,620
Combined into polygenic scores, the largest study so far explains about 12 to 16 percent of the differences in how much schooling people complete, well below twin study estimates for the same outcome.

188
00:14:05,820 --> 00:14:11,660
And when you compare siblings within the same family, the score's apparent effect shrinks by about half.

189
00:14:12,220 --> 00:14:15,980
That suggests part of what the scores capture comes from family background,

190
00:14:16,300 --> 00:14:20,100
such as the environment parents create and who partners with whom,

191
00:14:20,280 --> 00:14:23,320
rather than from a person's own genes acting directly.

192
00:14:23,760 --> 00:14:26,880
Meanwhile, the environment demonstrably moves scores.

193
00:14:27,220 --> 00:14:31,900
A 2018 meta-analysis by Stuart Ritchie and Tucker Draub,

194
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:36,140
drawing on natural experiments such as changes in the school-leaving age,

195
00:14:36,140 --> 00:14:42,020
estimated that each extra year of education raises IQ by roughly 1 to 5 points.

196
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:46,840
High heritability and big environmental effects at the same time.

197
00:14:46,860 --> 00:14:48,860
That sounds like a contradiction.

198
00:14:49,300 --> 00:14:52,240
It is not one, and the next finding shows why.

199
00:14:53,140 --> 00:14:58,120
The political scientist James Flynn documented that average IQ scores

200
00:14:58,120 --> 00:15:03,760
rose by roughly 3 points per decade through much of the 20th century in many countries.

201
00:15:04,320 --> 00:15:08,740
The gains were largest on abstract reasoning tests, like Raven's matrices,

202
00:15:09,160 --> 00:15:11,740
and smallest on vocabulary and arithmetic.

203
00:15:12,200 --> 00:15:14,860
That is far too fast for genetic change.

204
00:15:15,280 --> 00:15:17,860
Candidate causes include better nutrition and health,

205
00:15:18,180 --> 00:15:23,060
more schooling, smaller families, and what Flynn called scientific spectacles,

206
00:15:23,280 --> 00:15:26,780
a cultural habit of classifying and reasoning abstractly.

207
00:15:27,200 --> 00:15:29,800
He liked to cite interviews from the 1930s,

208
00:15:29,800 --> 00:15:35,900
in which the psychologist Alexander Luria posed simple syllogisms to unschooled farmers in Central

209
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:41,760
Asia, who often declined to reason about premises outside their own experience. Here, measurement

210
00:15:41,760 --> 00:15:48,880
invariance comes back. In 2004, Jelt Wickerts and colleagues tested five data sets, comparing

211
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:54,820
cohorts 10 to 60 years apart, and found that invariance failed in all of them. The tests were

212
00:15:54,820 --> 00:16:00,400
not measuring quite the same thing in earlier and later cohorts, so the gains cannot simply be read

213
00:16:00,400 --> 00:16:06,800
as gains in the underlying ability. And in some countries, the rise has reversed. Bernd Bratsberg

214
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:14,440
and Ole Roo-Jayborg analyzed the scores of about 730,000 Norwegian conscripts. Scores rose for men

215
00:16:14,440 --> 00:16:20,800
born up to the mid-1970s and then fell. Crucially, the decline shows up between brothers in the same

216
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:26,300
family, which rules out explanations based on which families have more children and points to

217
00:16:26,300 --> 00:16:32,440
the environment. That resolves the apparent paradox. Heritability describes variation within

218
00:16:32,440 --> 00:16:38,480
a population at one time. The Flynn effect is a shift of the whole population across time.

219
00:16:38,760 --> 00:16:44,060
A trait can be highly heritable and still move a lot when everyone's environment changes.

220
00:16:45,160 --> 00:16:49,240
That logic carries straight into the most contentious topic in this field,

221
00:16:51,440 --> 00:16:58,560
The key principle is that heritability within groups does not, by itself, tell you the causes of differences between groups.

222
00:16:58,860 --> 00:17:02,820
The geneticist Richard Lewontin's illustration is still the clearest.

223
00:17:03,260 --> 00:17:09,440
Take a bag of genetically varied seed and plant one handful in rich soil and another in poor soil.

224
00:17:09,660 --> 00:17:13,240
Within each field, differences in height may be largely genetic.

225
00:17:13,640 --> 00:17:19,840
But because both handfuls came from the same bag, the difference between the fields is entirely environmental.

226
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:26,080
The analogy shows that high heritability within groups is compatible with a wholly environmental

227
00:17:26,080 --> 00:17:32,600
gap between them. By itself, it cannot tell you which situation holds for any real human groups.

228
00:17:33,020 --> 00:17:39,860
On the evidence itself, the record runs like this. After the bell curve reignited public argument in

229
00:17:39,860 --> 00:17:48,000
1994, the American Psychological Association convened a task force. Its 1996 report concluded

230
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:52,740
that test scores predict important outcomes. It found that the average difference between

231
00:17:52,740 --> 00:17:57,880
black and white Americans, then about one standard deviation, did not come from obvious

232
00:17:57,880 --> 00:18:03,920
bias in the tests and did not simply reflect social class. Explanations based on culture

233
00:18:03,920 --> 00:18:09,220
and social position might be right, it said, but had little direct support, and there was

234
00:18:09,220 --> 00:18:15,460
no direct support at all for a genetic explanation. Its bottom line was that no one knew the cause.

235
00:18:15,860 --> 00:18:18,240
Here measurement invariance matters again.

236
00:18:18,580 --> 00:18:20,800
Unlike the gains across generations,

237
00:18:21,400 --> 00:18:23,660
studies comparing black and white Americans

238
00:18:23,660 --> 00:18:27,860
have mostly found that the tests measure the same abilities in both groups.

239
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,100
So the gap is not simply an artifact of the tests.

240
00:18:31,320 --> 00:18:34,420
That tells you what the gap is, not what causes it.

241
00:18:34,600 --> 00:18:40,220
In 2012, a review by Richard Nisbet, Flynn, Turkheimer, and others

242
00:18:40,220 --> 00:18:44,880
reported that the gap had narrowed by about a third of a standard deviation in recent decades.

243
00:18:45,460 --> 00:18:51,020
and its authors argued that group differences in IQ are best understood as environmental in origin.

244
00:18:51,560 --> 00:18:56,200
Others, such as Charles Murray, accepted some narrowing over the century,

245
00:18:56,400 --> 00:19:02,520
but argued that it had stalled by the late 1980s. And some researchers, most prominently Arthur

246
00:19:02,520 --> 00:19:08,120
Jensen and Philippe Rushton, argued that genes explain part of the gap, pointing to indirect

247
00:19:08,120 --> 00:19:13,820
evidence such as heritability and adoption studies. Critics reply that indirect evidence

248
00:19:15,460 --> 00:19:21,000
separate genetic from environmental causes. Opinion among researchers has been divided,

249
00:19:21,240 --> 00:19:26,940
and no direct evidence has settled the question. Today's genetic tools cannot settle it either.

250
00:19:27,680 --> 00:19:33,540
Polygenic scores, built mostly from people of European ancestry, lose much of their accuracy

251
00:19:33,540 --> 00:19:39,180
in other populations, and even within one population, they partly pick up family environment.

252
00:19:39,860 --> 00:19:42,260
International comparisons have a further problem.

253
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:45,680
A widely cited set of national IQ estimates,

254
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:48,820
compiled by Richard Lynn and Tatu von Hanen,

255
00:19:49,220 --> 00:19:52,140
rests in many countries on one or two small samples

256
00:19:52,140 --> 00:19:54,840
that were never designed to represent the whole country.

257
00:19:55,540 --> 00:19:58,520
In 2010, Wick Hertz and colleagues

258
00:19:58,520 --> 00:20:00,540
reviewed the African data systematically,

259
00:20:00,880 --> 00:20:03,680
with explicit rules for which samples to include.

260
00:20:03,880 --> 00:20:07,180
Their average came out roughly 12 points higher than Lynn's,

261
00:20:07,320 --> 00:20:09,060
though still below British norms.

262
00:20:09,720 --> 00:20:14,120
They warned that Lynn's figures rested on unsystematic choices of samples,

263
00:20:14,340 --> 00:20:19,720
and that whether the tests measure the same thing in those populations still needed proper study.

264
00:20:19,960 --> 00:20:25,200
Papers built on the dataset are now being re-examined, and at least three have been retracted,

265
00:20:25,420 --> 00:20:31,420
most recently one in a Royal Society journal in 2024 and another in 2025.

266
00:20:32,140 --> 00:20:35,160
Countries really do differ on international school assessments,

267
00:20:35,160 --> 00:20:38,780
but how much of that reflects the same underlying ability

268
00:20:38,780 --> 00:20:42,020
and why the differences exist are separate questions.

269
00:20:42,380 --> 00:20:47,220
And the Flynn effect is a reminder that national averages move as countries develop.

270
00:20:48,530 --> 00:20:50,580
Two neighboring ideas, briefly.

271
00:20:50,900 --> 00:20:55,400
Howard Gardner's multiple intelligences are hugely popular in education,

272
00:20:55,660 --> 00:20:59,660
but when researchers built measures of the supposedly separate intelligences,

273
00:20:59,980 --> 00:21:02,800
most of them loaded on G and on each other.

274
00:21:03,520 --> 00:21:05,760
Rationality is the more interesting case.

275
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:11,860
The psychologist Keith Stanovich coined the word dysrationalia for smart people who reason badly.

276
00:21:12,640 --> 00:21:19,120
Resisting a tempting but wrong intuition turns out to be at least partly a different skill from raw reasoning power,

277
00:21:19,360 --> 00:21:23,780
though the two are related, and standard intelligence tests largely miss it.

278
00:21:25,030 --> 00:21:30,940
So if someone tells me a language model has an IQ of 130, what should I ask?

279
00:21:32,800 --> 00:21:38,780
is supposed to measure. Human abilities really do covery, and the general factor that summarizes

280
00:21:38,780 --> 00:21:44,980
them predicts real outcomes, but at least four explanations fit the same data, and the factor's

281
00:21:44,980 --> 00:21:50,600
biological meaning is still open. Then ask whether the test means the same thing for that system

282
00:21:50,600 --> 00:21:55,240
as for the people it was built for, which is the invariance question in a new setting.

283
00:21:55,440 --> 00:21:59,540
And keep in mind how easily the numbers people fight about are misread.

284
00:22:00,280 --> 00:22:03,060
Heritability describes populations, not persons.

285
00:22:03,420 --> 00:22:06,460
It coexists with large environmental effects.

286
00:22:06,720 --> 00:22:10,300
And by itself, it cannot explain differences between groups.

287
00:22:10,600 --> 00:22:16,180
Most of all, notice that everything in this episode has been about how humans compare with other humans.

288
00:22:16,460 --> 00:22:24,520
A test built for schoolchildren tells you little, by itself, about what a crow, an octopus, or a language model has or lacks.

289
00:22:24,880 --> 00:22:27,300
For that, we leave the human case behind.

290
00:22:27,300 --> 00:22:35,900
Next episode, minds and other bodies from a crow that bent a wire into a hook to cells that solve problems without any brain at all.

